  "full_response": "The CAP theorem, also known as Brewer's theorem, is a fundamental principle in distributed computing that outlines the trade-offs involved when designing a distributed database system. The theorem states that it is impossible for a distributed data store to simultaneously provide more than two of the following three guarantees:\n\n1. **Consistency (C):** Every read operation receives the most recent write for a given piece of data. In other words, all nodes see the same data at the same time, which ensures consistency across the distributed system.\n\n2. **Availability (A):** Every request (read or write) receives a non-error response, without guaranteeing that it contains the most recent write. This property ensures that the system is up and running, and can always respond to requests, even if some nodes are down.\n\n3. **Partition Tolerance (P):** The system continues to operate despite arbitrary partitioning due to network failures. That is, the system can continue to function even if parts of the system are unable to communicate with each other due to network issues.\n\n### Implications for Distributed Database Design\n\nWhen designing a distributed database, you must decide which two of the three properties—consistency, availability, and partition tolerance—the system should prioritize, as achieving all three simultaneously is not feasible:\n\n1. **Consistency and Partition Tolerance (CP):**\n   - Systems that prioritize CP will ensure consistency and partition tolerance but may sacrifice availability. \n   - Example: A system might be unable to process write requests or some read requests if a network partition occurs, so it chooses to ensure that all available data remains consistent.\n   - Use case: Financial systems that need to ensure accurate and consistent data, even if it means being temporarily unavailable during a network partition.\n\n2. **Availability and Partition Tolerance (AP):**\n   - Systems that choose AP will ensure availability and partition tolerance but might sacrifice consistency. \n   - Example: Systems allow reads and writes to proceed even during partitions, but the data might be stale or inconsistent across partitions.\n   - Use case: Social media platforms where it's more important for the system to remain available and responsive, even at the cost of temporary inconsistencies.\n\n3. **Consistency and Availability (CA):**\n   - In theory, prioritizing CA without partition tolerance could work in a distributed setup if there are never any network partitions, which is generally not practical in real-world scenarios that can experience network splits.\n   - Example: Such systems are feasible in environments with no network failures.\n   - Use case: Non-distributed databases or",
